description:
FILED s543q (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-28, CODE 6a1301ed = MAIN-READY-114 + records, v2-16500) by the Rosetta census B (docs/rosetta-plan.md section 6b; #2104).

THE SHAPE (neutral reproducer, run on the tree, perl 5.40.3 vs PCL):

    use builtin qw(indexed);
    no warnings;
    my @pairs = indexed("x", "y");
    print "@pairs\n";

    perl: 0 x 1 y
    PCL : Undefined subroutine &main::indexed called at ... line 3.   (exit 255)

    The qualified call fails the same way: `builtin::indexed("a", "b")` dies "Undefined subroutine &BUILTIN::indexed".

WHAT THE RUNTIME HAS (`defined &builtin::NAME`, probed for perl 5.40.3's list): true false is_bool weaken unweaken is_weak blessed refaddr reftype ceil floor trim stringify created_as_string created_as_number are there; MISSING: indexed, inf, nan, is_tainted, load_module, export_lexically -- `use builtin qw(inf nan); print inf` dies "Undefined subroutine &main::inf" too (probed: perl prints inf; the parser registers their `()` prototype, #3242, but the runtime has no function).  Only `indexed` is met by a Rosetta program.
WHERE: `p-import-builtins` (cl/pcl-runtime.lisp) aliases each named function from the BUILTIN package and SKIPS a name it does not define ("perl's own list grows per version"), so the `use` succeeds and the call dies later; perl dies at compile time for an unknown name and has `indexed` since 5.36.  The function itself is `(i0, v0, i1, v1, ...)` over its argument list (in scalar context the count of that list, perl warns).

CHEAP DISCRIMINATING MEASUREMENT (taken): with `sub indexed { my $i = 0; map { ($i++, $_) } @_ }` in place of the import, 0294 is byte-identical to perl (its only cause); 0119 then still differs by #650 (its import list is `use builtin <indexed floor>`) and #5560 (`\K`), and with all three patched it is identical too.  The `for my ($k, $v) (LIST)` loops of both programs (5.36 for_list) work.

PROGRAMS: 0294-Countdown, 0119-Bifid-cipher.
